      "textEn": "368\n\nThose who are intoxicated by their own words, and are in search of Krishna. Leela Bhagavatastastahyanuchakrastadatmikah. 14. Meaning of the word\n\nThus—in this way; Unmatta—frenzied; Vachah—speaking; Gopiya—Gopis; Krishna-Anveshan—searching for Krishna;\nKatraha—confused about what to do; Leela—divine plays; Bhagavata—of the Lord; Taah Taah—each of them; Hi—indeed;\nAnuchakrushah—imitated; Tat-Atmikaah—being absorbed in their thoughts. After saying these words, the Gopis, confused about their duties, while searching for Krishna,\nbecame completely absorbed in his thoughts and began to imitate his various pastimes. Kasyachitpūtanāyantyāha Kṛṣṇāyantyāpiḍbhatstanam. Tokayitva rudatyanya padahanshakatayitim. 15. Meaning of the word:\n\nKasyachit—One of the Gopis; Putnayantyah—The one who became Putana; Krishnayanti—The other one who became Krishna; Apibat—Drank;\nStanam—To the breast; Tokayitva—Becoming a baby; Rudati—Shouted; Anya—The other one; Pada—With her feet; Ahn—Hit;\nShakatayitim—The one who became the cart.\n.\n\nOne Gopi imitated Putana, while the other child became Krishna, and she first\nstarted breastfeeding Vali. Other Gopis, imitating the weeping of Shishu Krishna, that\nGopi struck the feet, who was playing the role of Shakatasur. Daityayitva jaharanyameko krishnabhabhavanam. The rangy-bellied crows are chattering their beaks. 16. Meaning of the word\n\nDaityayitva—Becoming a demon; Jahar—Took away; Anyam—To another Gopi; Eka—One Gopi; Krishna-Arbh—Of the child Krishna; Bhavanam—Having a feeling; Ringyam Aas—Started crawling; Ka Api—One of them; Adgri—His\nTwo legs; Karshanti—Crawling; Ghosh—Of the peg; Nishwasnaih—Sounding..\n\nOne Gopi became Trunavarta, and she took the other Gopi, who had become the child Krishna, far away.\nWhile another Gopi started crawling, causing her feet to make a sound as she dragged them. Meaning: The Gopis started imitating all the pastimes of Krishna, starting from his childhood. Krishnaramayite d